{
  "about": "Reading JSON into records through Clarity.Formats (docs/FORMATS-PLAN.md; DESIGN 5.23): `read Order from json text` and `read list<Order> from json text`, with the option `at` (`\"data.items\"`) to start inside. The library fits what is read to what the program wants (`kind`: a record or a list of records, with its entity, fields and base) before the stack's own decoder reads the result: the text is parsed, `at` walks object names (dots between them), then each object is fitted to the entity's fields. A field is found by its name, else by its name ignoring case, underscores and hyphens (credit_limit, creditLimit and Credit-Limit are one). Fields the JSON leaves out, or gives as null, keep the record's defaults (`base`, a new record as Clarity writes it), and names the entity does not have are ignored; so is `id`, as the record is new. Each kind takes: text a string, a number or a bool (written as JSON writes it); integer a whole number or a string of one; decimal (and money) a number or a string of one, written as exact text; bool true or false; date a string of the date (a date and time keeps its date); datetime a string `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM` or with seconds, a fraction of a second and a `Z` left out; enum a string of one of its values, any case; values a list whose items fit `item`; record an object, fitted to the fields and base of its own entity (`entity`, `fields`, `base` on the field), and records a list of such objects, each fitted alike: records inside records, which an entity of a web API has (DESIGN 5.25); other (a reference, a picture, a file, a list of stored records) is never read. An error inside a nested object or list says where: `field 'lines' of Order, item 2: ` before the message about the inner record. `fitted` is the record or list that results; `error` the message a reading that does not fit gives, word for word. Numbers are taken from the text they were written in, never through a floating point number: a decimal keeps every digit and an exponent is written out, a whole number is any 64-bit integer (also written with a zero fraction or an exponent), strings of numbers may have spaces around them, a number read as text keeps how it was written, and of names that match ignoring case, underscores, hyphens and spaces the first written counts.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "an object fits its fields, by name and by name ignoring case and underscores",
      "kind": {
        "kind": "record",
        "entity": "Order",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "number",
            "kind": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "credit_limit",
            "kind": "decimal"
          },
          {
            "name": "lines",
            "kind": "integer"
          },
          {
            "name": "paid",
            "kind": "bool"
          }
        ],
        "base": {
          "id": 0,
          "number": "",
          "credit_limit": "0",
          "lines": 0,
          "paid": false
        }
      },
      "input": "{\"number\": \"A-12\", \"creditLimit\": 1999.5, \"LINES\": 3, \"paid\": true, \"extra\": [1, 2]}",
      "fitted": {
        "id": 0,
        "number": "A-12",
        "credit_limit": "1999.5",
        "lines": 3,
        "paid": true
      }
    },
